A||||3||Patrick Horne||Hubs/Spindles...?||||1||
Z||000000||Patrick Horne||11-24-2001||01:32 PM||||Okay, can somebody tell me EXACTLY what I need to get to mate the Northstar 4T80E transmission up to the stock '86 2M4 Fiero wheels, as in year/make/model car and what parts from each car?  <BR>Just want something that I can take to a junkyard or Advance Auto and say "I need these parts..."<P>Many thanks<P>------------------<BR>Patrick Horne||||reg||1
Z||000001||fieroX||11-24-2001||03:33 PM||||making the transmission fit wont be that big of a deal.  Mating the transmission to the wheels will be a project.  What you need is the axles from the car the tranny came from originally.  You are going to be using the inner section of the axle on the driver side, with the stock fiero axle and cv.  On the passenge side you may need to have a custom axle made.  If the car the tranny came from has aproximately the same distance between the hubs you can use the axle on the passenger side.  <BR>Heres what you have to do.  #1 you are going to be using the 4t80e inner, cv, axle and star.  When you dissassemble the outer section of the cv shaft there will be a splined section of the axle, a 6 pointed star connected to the axle, 6 ball bearings, and a bearing cage.  You are going to have to dissassemble the fiero axle also.  You are going to use the star from the 4t80e and the bearings, cage and outer housing from the fiero.  Put it all back together and use alot of grease.  <BR>We had to do this on my friends Lumina with the L67 swap, it clicks around corners, but luckily in a fiero it doesnt have to bend in that way to make them click.  His axles have worked for thousands of miles.  Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick Horne||11-24-2001||03:53 PM||||So nothing has to be done about buying new hubs or spindles?  Just rebuild the axles to fit each other...??<P>------------------<BR>Patrick Horne||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroX||11-24-2001||09:39 PM||||Yup, thats by far the easiest, most reliable, most economical and most practical way you can go about doing it.||||reg||1
